    "created_at": "2018-02-02T17:47:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-02-02T18:02:55Z",
    "body": "@domenic Have certainly not worked out all of the details, nor even composed a POC specific to parsing a `<script type=\"shell\">` element. Just sharing a concept. It took quite some time for this user to gather the proper usage of Native Messaging protocol for the ability to call a single binary from a Chrome App - without starting a local server and `GET` or `POST`.\r\n\r\nHTML has used `type` other than `\"application/javascript\"` in the past; i.e.g. [18 Scripts](https://www.w3.org/TR/html4/interact/scripts.html), and am curious if that implementation could be revisited within the current era of HTML.\r\n\r\nIf the concept is not viable at `<script>` element whatsoever will explore other options to run local commands within a sandboxed environment from HTML without using an \"app\", extension or local server, then formatting the output for the ability to `import` the result of the command as a JavaScript `module`.\r\n\r\nIs the concept not viable?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
